{"items":[{"tags":["python","fastapi","starlette"],"question_score":12,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":70007582,"item_type":"question","score":12,"last_activity_date":1780582672,"creation_date":1637164366,"body":"I was trying to write a FastAPI POST method as:\n\nWhen I do a Postman call to  I get the expected result, but if the call is changed to  I get 405 Method Not Allowed.\nAccording to FastAPI docs, the trailing slashes shouldn&#39;t matter, so ideally:\n\nwith Postman calls:\n\n\n\n\nall the above 4 combinations should give the same result. What am I doing wrong?\nVersions:\n\nFastAPI 0.63\nStarlette 0.13.6\n\n","excerpt":"/method/<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>/ I get the expected result, but if the call is changed to .../method/<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> I get 405 Method Not Allowed. &hellip; According to FastAPI docs, the trailing slashes shouldn&#39;t matter, so ideally:\n@app.post(&#39;/method/<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>/&#39;, tags=[&#39;method/<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>&#39;])\n@app.post(&#39;/method/<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>&#39;, tags=[&#39;method/<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>&#39;])\n\nwith &hellip; ","title":"POST method call with and without trailing slashes"},{"tags":["php","image","gd"],"question_score":15,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79933146,"is_answered":false,"question_id":14342355,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1777388733,"creation_date":1777388391,"body":"I&#39;ve recently gone through some trial and error with Tif to PNG using Imagick. I found some code which mentioned that tif files contain multipe images  - you can use this function\n\nI&#39;ve found that all the Tif&#39;s I tested with had 2 images and the 2nd one gives the detail you want.\n\nI also discovered that if you want to convert TIF to JPG - the results are poor you lose a lot of detail going straight from tif &gt; jpg.\nYou can great results if you go from tif &gt; png &gt; jpg.\nHope this helps someone else\n","excerpt":"$<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = str_replace($pathinfo[&#39;extension&#39;],&quot;png&quot;,$base_path. &hellip; ;             // keep aspect ratio\n        $image-&gt;setImageFormat(&#39;png&#39;);              // or &#39;jpeg&#39;\n        $image-&gt;setImageCompressionQuality(70);     // JPEG quality\n        $image-&gt;writeImage($<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Convert tiff to jpg in php?"},{"tags":["python","snyk"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79378676,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1770603107,"creation_date":1737566406,"body":"I need to read and write JSON files in my project, and Snyk is flagging a &quot;Path Traversal Vulnerability&quot; in my code. Initially, I was dynamically constructing file paths using user input (command line parameters), which I understand can be a security risk. For example:\n\nSince this approach allowed filenames to be constructed based on input values, it was getting flagged as a path traversal vulnerability.\nTo fix this, I completely removed dynamic file path construction and replaced it with a hardcoded dictionary lookup.\n\nNow, the file path is strictly determined by a dictionary lookup, with no string concatenation or formatting based on user input:\n\nSince &#39;category&#39; and &#39;subtype can only be valid keys from the dictionary, there is no way for a user to inject arbitrary file paths or &#39;../&#39; traversal attacks.\nEven after making this change, Snyk still flags this as a path traversal vulnerability, claiming that untrusted input flows into &#39;open(...)&#39;.\nI’ve tried:\n\nEnsuring all file paths are hardcoded in a dictionary.\nNormalizing paths and checking for traversal attempts.\nAdding a Snyk ignore comment (&#39;# SNYK ignore PathTraversal&#39;), but it\nstill flags the issue. (ChatGPT&#39;s suggestion)\n\nDespite these changes, Snyk keeps detecting a vulnerability.\nWhy is Snyk still flagging this as a path traversal vulnerability if all file paths are predefined in a dictionary and cannot be manipulated? Is there an additional way to explicitly prove to Snyk that this is not a security risk?\nThanks in advance!\n","excerpt":"&quot;subtype_y&quot;: &quot;C:/mytrustedfolder/b/y_columns.json&quot;,\n    }\n}\n\nNow, the file path is strictly determined by a dictionary lookup, with no string concatenation or formatting based on user input:\n<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = file_mapping[category][subtype]\n\nwith open(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>, &#39;r&#39;, encoding=&#39;utf-8&#39;) as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\n\nSince &#39;category&#39; and &#39;subtype can only be valid keys from the dictionary, there is no way &hellip; ","title":"How to resolve Snyk Path Traversal Warning?"},{"tags":["pyspark","databricks","spss-files"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79843276,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1765391974,"creation_date":1765388203,"body":"I’m reading various file types in Databricks using Spark — including PDF, DOCX, PPTX, XLSX, and CSV.\nSome inputs are ZIP archives that contain multiple files, including SPSS  files.\nMy workflow is:\n\nDetect if the input file is a ZIP.\n\nExtract it to a temporary directory.\n\nIterate through the extracted files.\n\nBased on each file’s extension, call the appropriate reader function.\n\n\nEverything works correctly except for the  files.\nI use code that normally reads SPSS files,\n\nbut in this case Spark fails and only logs an error. I verified that the  files exist after extraction and their sizes are correct, so the files themselves seem fine.\n\nIt looks like Spark/Databricks has trouble reading SPSS  files extracted from ZIP archives.\nAny guidance would be appreciated.\nHere is the output log for review:\n\nI also remove  flag but nothing changed.\nUpdate 1\nIf I use the code below instead, it runs successfully, but it’s very slow:\nchange\n\nto\n\nand change for block from\n\nto\n\n\n","excerpt":"directory and files are deleted automatically\n    return extracted_zip_chunks\n\n\n\nroot_path = &quot;gs://google-cloud-path/ABC/&quot;\nall_files = list_files_in_directory(root_path)  # This is your real list\n<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Spark (Databricks) fails to read SPSS .sav files extracted from ZIP"},{"tags":["python","algorithm","graph-theory","chinese-postman"],"question_score":4,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78612979,"item_type":"question","score":4,"last_activity_date":1754250874,"creation_date":1718198939,"body":"I&#39;m looking to collect 360 imagery in a given area by driving all the streets in it, trying to optimize the route (a variant of the Chinese Postman Problem - CPP).\nI am trying to use this nice code, Every Street, which solves the original CPP, so I don&#39;t need to fully develop a solution. In summary, this is what the original code does:\n\nget all pedestrian-allowed roads in a given area out of OpenStreetMap\n\napply an algorithm proposed by Edmonds, J. and Johnson, which gives the optimal solution. This algorithm is applied inside the network package, developed by same author.\n\n\nThe code works pretty fine for the original problem, but I can&#39;t adapt to my problem. There are 2 adjustments I think I need to apply in the original code:\n\nadjust the ways to only obtain the drivable roads (exclude pedestrian paths etc)\nmake the graph directed\n\nThe code uses OSMnx package, so the first part I believe is easily solved, using the parameter :\n\nThe original code also have this line, which I removed:\n\nIt seems the graph is oriented, but in the end, the resulted GPX file still don&#39;t respect the direction of the streets. OpenStreetMap is correctly mapped, and I inserted some lines in the middle of the code to check if the graph is oriented, which it is confirmed.\nIs there anything I&#39;m missing? I also checked the network package, which seems ready to deal with a directed graph. This is a discussion he had some time ago, without solution.\nYou can see the original code here, and my adapted code here:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn this last section, at , I also added , but then it throws an error:\n\n","excerpt":"hierholzer(network)\nconverted_eulerian_path = convert_integer_path2osmnx_nodes(eulerian_path, graph.nodes())\ndouble_edge_heap = get_double_edge_heap(org_graph)\n\n# Finds the final path with edge IDs\n<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = convert_path(graph, converted_eulerian_path, double_edge_heap)\ncoordinates_path = convert_final_path_to_coordinates(org_graph, <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>)\n\neccentricity = nx.eccentricity(graph)\ncenter = nx.center(graph &hellip; ","title":"How to drive every street in a given area?"},{"tags":["python","excel","win32com"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79644222,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1748637796,"creation_date":1748533900,"body":"I&#39;m trying to run a script that reads a txt file that contains a list of file paths to Excel files, and save each one as a pdf in a specified directory. I can get it to read the txt file  and parse the list of file paths no problem, but it runs into an error when it tries to save the file as a pdf.\nHere&#39;s my save pdfs function that runs into an error:\n\nAnd whenever I run this, I get the following error for each of the files:\n\nI don&#39;t have Excel running when I start the script and I&#39;ve checked the files themselves and nobody else has the file open already. If anyone else has run into a similar situation, please let me know what you did to fix it.\nEdit: Here&#39;s the full script, and the file it asks for is a txt file that consists of the full file paths to each individual file, since they&#39;re mostly in different directories. I populated my test file by selecting the files, right clicking, and selecting &quot;Copy as path&quot; and pasting into the text file.\nI also did make the changes that Panagiotis Kanavos suggested about moving the opening and quitting of Excel outside of the loop, thank you for the advice!\n\nFor testing purposes, the text file&#39;s contents look like this:\n\nBut in practice will be a list of file paths in different directories on the network.\nAs a side note, everything seems to be fine with opening the Excel files, I tested it with commenting out the part where it closes each file and quits Excel and each file opens successfully, it&#39;s just an issue with it then trying to save the files as PDFs.\n---Second Edit---\nI was able to get it working using pathlib like Panagiotis Kanavos suggested. Here&#39;s the fully working script that I have going:\n\n","excerpt":"excel.Visible = False\n    excel.Application.DisplayAlerts = False\n\n    for line_item, source_file_path in enumerate(files_list, start=1):\n        print(line_item, f&#39;{source_file_path}&#39;)\n        <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = export_folder / (str(line_item).zfill(2) + &#39;.pdf&#39;)\n        print(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>)\n        try:\n            wb = excel.Workbooks.Open(source_file_path, ReadOnly=True)\n            work_sheets = wb.Worksheets &hellip; ","title":"Saving Excel files as PDF using win32com python library and getting error"},{"tags":["java","unit-testing","evosuite"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79502570,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1741749416,"creation_date":1741749416,"body":"I am trying to write a program for java unit test generation (Coding language is python though).\nSince I am using Python, I figured using EvoSuite&#39;s command line would be the easiest way to integrate with it.\nI want to leverage EvoSuite&#39;s Mutation Assertion Generation section of the framework, in order to make my CURRENT unit test more robust.\nPractically, I made a class that given:\n\nFolder with the .class files of both source code, and unit test.\nThe path to the .java file (To extract the name and package)\nAn output directory.\nI wanted it to inject assertions to the existing tests.\nI used JDK 1.8.0_202 for compiling the files, and for using the tools.jar file.\nEvoSuite .jar version is 1.0.6. Here is my implementation:\n\n\nWhile this class does work and generate a unit test, it does NOT use my existing unit test. Is there a way to provide EvoSuite with an existing unit test for enhancements?\n","excerpt":"self.package_name else &quot;&quot;\n        generated_test_file = os.path.join(generated_test_dir, package_path, f&quot;{self.java_file_name}_ESTest.java&quot;)\n\n        if os.path.exists(generated_test_file):\n            <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = os.path.join(self.output_dir, os.path.basename(generated_test_file))\n            shutil.move(generated_test_file, <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>)\n            print(f&quot;Modified test saved to: {<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>}&quot;)\n        else: &hellip; ","title":"Using EvoSuite&#39;s Mutation assertion generation to enhance an existing unit test"},{"tags":["django","heroku","amazon-s3"],"question_score":12,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":43529912,"item_type":"question","score":12,"last_activity_date":1739172982,"creation_date":1492722394,"body":"I&#39;ve been through the ringer getting my Django app setup on Heroku using Amazon s3 to host the static and media files.  I&#39;ve been following this guide https://www.caktusgroup.com/blog/2014/11/10/Using-Amazon-S3-to-store-your-Django-sites-static-and-media-files/ and what seems like thousands of other resources, collectstatic has worked and heroku is deploying it - but displays a 400 error.  When I try and run it locally i get more info:\n\n\n\nThis is the line that gets highlighted:\n\n\n\nI can load the static files direct from the URL if I grab it from my s3 admin panel so I figured it wasn&#39;t a bucket policy issue, I&#39;ve messed around with https / http options but no joy.  So I figure it must be the wrong path is being called somehow in the code - i just can&#39;t see where! \n\nAny help much appreciated, I don&#39;t think I&#39;ve blinked for about 4 hours straight.\n\nTraceback:\n\n\n\nsettings.py\n\n\n\nlocal_settings.py\n\n\n\ncustom_storages.py\n\n\n\n\n\nEDIT:\n\n\n\nI managed to get it working by messing around with various values in the settings.py, but is still not right.\n\nHere is all the stuff relating to static and media paths\n\n\n\nHere is custom_storage that I&#39;m importing:\n\n\n\nNow, if i comment out\n\n\n\nIt will load the static files from S3 and all is fine, BUT collectstatic fails.  If I uncomment that line, collectstatic works but it gives an error when I try and load the site.  The error is:\n\n\n\nSo clearly something is up with that custom_storage part but I have no idea what :/\n","excerpt":"The error is:\n\n # Ensure <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> starts with base_path and that the next character after\n    # the final path is &#39;/&#39; (or nothing, in which case <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> must be\n    # equal to base_path). &hellip; base_path_len = len(base_path)\n    if (not final_path.startswith(base_path) or\n            <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>[base_path_len:base_path_len + 1] not in (&#39;&#39;, &#39;/&#39;)):\n        raise ValueError(&#39;the joined path is located &hellip; ","title":"&#39;Suspicious Operation: Attempted access to &quot;&quot; denied&#39; while loading static files"},{"tags":["java","multithreading","javafx","task"],"question_score":-3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":79336868,"item_type":"question","score":-3,"last_activity_date":1736624312,"creation_date":1736271411,"body":"In a distinct , a fresh  initiates a  via a  object to save the data in Excel or JSON format.\nThe getFileSavingDialogue method starts the file chooser&#39;s save dialogue utilizing  to handle it on the  as it&#39;s a UI operation\nThis function only returns the file the user chooses after clicking the  of the dialogue. To facilitate this, an  is used. The loop persists until a file is selected and referenced by fileRef.\n\nThis is the call method of the Task:\n\nThis is the event handler of the button that initiated the saving process:\n\nMy issue is that if the user closes that dialogue window, both  and  in the view will be disabled and never be enabled again because the task started and will never finish or be canceled.\nHow can I detect that the dialogue window was closed and reenable the buttons again?\n","excerpt":"System.out.println(e.getMessage());\n            }\n        }\n\n        return fileRef.get();\n    }\n\nThis is the call method of the Task:\n@Override\n    protected Void call() {\n        <span class=\"highlight\">FINAL_PATH</span> &hellip; ","title":"Anticapte FileChooser closing to abort a the runing task"},{"tags":["python","selenium-chromedriver","timeoutexception"],"question_score":-1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":79037346,"item_type":"question","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1727778186,"creation_date":1727642238,"body":"Issue Description:\n\nI am trying to automate a process where I can visit a website, hover over the menu navigation bar and click on each navigation category options from tier 1 dropdown, visit that page and scrape product details of top 20 products on that page and put it in an excel file. If that page does not contain any product, the script will continue scrolling down till it reaches the end of the page and if no product-div is found, it will go back to the top of the page and click on the next category in the navigation panel\n\nI am working with Selenium (using python) for this. I have attached my code below.\nscroll_and_click_view_more function is for scrolling down the page, prod_vitals function is for scraping product details specific to each page, and prod_count function is for extracting total count of products on each page and creating a summary of all pages.\nError Description:\n\nWhen I run the below code, every function is working fine except one. The first page that this code is scrolling down, does not have any product details. The script will scroll down the entire page, print no product tiles found on that page and then is supposed to click on the next category but for some reason it can&#39;t click on the next category in the path. It throws timeout exception error and clicks on the next category which is working fine again. This website has two categories where there is no product tile present and for both of these pages, the script is unable to click on the next category available. I am attaching a screenshot of the error.\n\nOutput of my code:\n\n\nIf you look at the output, in the first line, it prints all the navigation categories available on the site. After that, the script visits all the URLs in that array is able to click on all the URLs except the second and eighth one.\nFYI, the first and seventh category does not contain any product tile on that page. Rest all the links are clickable.\nThe clicking on each category and iterating over the loop is taken care inside the WebScraper class.\n\nResolution Steps:\n\nI have tried adding time.sleep() in between the actions but still this doesn&#39;t work. I also added a step where it is taking a screenshot when timeout exception is happening, I can see the category is visible on screen but still it is not clickable.\n\nI am attaching a screenshot of the output on terminal.\n\nI am attaching my code below:\n\nPlease find my updated code below as per @mehdi-ahmadi&#39;s comment and along with it the output and issues I am facing now\n\nI initially tried with your first option\nbut that was not working fine so decided to change the logic instead\nand tried with second option by getting anchor from nav each time.\nWith this logic, the second link is clickable now (&#39;/shop/new/women&#39;).\nHowever, the last link is again getting timeout exception and not able\nto click on it(/shop/coachreloved/coach-reloved).\n\nPlease find the output below:\n\nI am attaching my updated class also below. Can you please help?\n\n","excerpt":"#print(&quot;Temporary path&quot;,temp_path)\n#<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> = os.path.join(custom_path, &quot;chromedriver.exe&quot;)      # constructs and stores the full path to the ChromeDriver executable in the custom directory. &hellip; #shutil.move(temp_path, <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>)                              # Moves the ChromeDriver executable from the temporary directory to the custom directory. &hellip; ","title":"Not able to click on element in a Selenium using Python automation script"},{"tags":["include","jekyll"],"question_score":26,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":75420946,"is_answered":false,"question_id":39734133,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1722596681,"creation_date":1676126753,"body":"None of the plugins referenced in other answers worked.\nSo here&#39;s my plugin in 20 lines that did work for me:\n\nCreate a folder called  in the root of your project if you don&#39;t already have one. Then place a file there with this content. Then restart Jekyll.\nNow in your pages, layouts and includes you can do this:\n\nor this:\n\nor this:\n\nThey&#39;re all the same.\nIf you have a post with front matter like this:\n\nYou can then do this:\n\nIf you have a post with front matter like this:\n\nYou can then do this:\n\nAfter 7 years I find it&#39;s a bit mindboggling that such a basic use-case is still unsupported by Jekyll. I wanted exactly the same as Brad. I wanted to have a code-snippet in a single place, that I could both  and make available through an . For whoever is interested, this is a simplified version of what I&#39;m using:\n\nVery odd that we would need a custom plugin for this.\n","excerpt":"markup = markup.strip\n  end\n\n  def render(context)\n    expanded_path = Liquid::Template.parse(@markup).render(context)\n    root_path = File.expand_path(context.registers[:site].config[&#39;source&#39;])\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; = File.join(root_path, expanded_path)\n    read_file(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>, context)\n  end\n\n  def read_file(path, context)\n    file_read_opts = context.registers[:site].file_read_opts\n    File.read(path, **file_read_opts &hellip; ","title":"Jekyll: Include a file from directory outside of _includes"},{"tags":["c++","algorithm","grid","graph-theory","breadth-first-search"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":78451963,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78451221,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1715221897,"creation_date":1715221897,"body":"You could simply modify your code to use  (which gives the path from A to B reversed), then iterate over  in reverse.\n\nAlternatively, use  instead of  to store the path as it has constant time .\n\n","excerpt":"You could simply modify your code to use push_back (which gives the path from A to B reversed), then iterate over <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> in reverse. &hellip; #include &lt;deque&gt;\n// ...\ndeque&lt;char&gt; <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>;\n// example:\nfinal_path.push_front(&#39;R&#39;); &hellip; ","title":"BFS implementation takes too much time for CSES labyrinth problem"},{"tags":["c++","algorithm","grid","graph-theory","breadth-first-search"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":78451221,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1715221897,"creation_date":1715203713,"body":"I am trying to solve the CSES Labyrinth problem:\n\nYou are given a map of a labyrinth, and your task is to find a path from start to end. You can walk left, right, up and down.\nInput\nThe first input line has two integers n and m: the height and width of the map.\nThen there are n lines of m characters describing the labyrinth. Each character is  (floor),  (wall),  (start), or  (end). There is exactly one  and one  in the input.\nOutput\nFirst print &quot;YES&quot;, if there is a path, and &quot;NO&quot; otherwise.\nIf there is a path, print the length of the shortest such path and its description as a string consisting of characters  (left),  (right),  (up), and  (down). You can print any valid solution.\nConstraints\n1 ≤ &#119899;,&#119898; ≤ 1000\nExample\nInput:\n\nOutput:\n\n\nI implemented a BFS algorithm. It solves all the test cases except one. In one of the test cases I get a time limit exceeded (TLE) error.\nI have tried various approaches and watched many solutions on youtube as well, but there isn&#39;t anything standing out in those solutions that can resolve my problem or I might be missing something. Can someone spot where I am going wrong?\nMy code:\n\n","excerpt":"queue&gt;\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nbool vis[1001][1001];\nchar path[1001][1001];\nqueue&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt; q;\nvector&lt;pair&lt;int, int&gt;&gt; moves = {\n    {1, 0},\n    {-1, 0},\n    {0, 1},\n    {0, -1}};\nvector&lt;char&gt; <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; {\n                vis[i][j] = true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (bfs(sx, sy))\n    {\n        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;YES&quot; &lt;&lt; endl\n            &lt;&lt; final_path.size() &lt;&lt; endl;\n        for (auto i : <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"BFS implementation takes too much time for CSES labyrinth problem"},{"tags":["c++","algorithm","grid","graph-theory","breadth-first-search"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":78451295,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78451221,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1715204958,"creation_date":1715204958,"body":" is not efficient, it has a O(&#119899;) time complexity where &#119899; is the size of . As you need to call this repeatedly, it amounts to a time complexity of O(&#119899;&#178;) where &#119899; is the length of the found path.\nFor better efficiency, start your search from B and find A, so in the opposite direction. Then you can use  which has better efficiency -- amortised O(1) in your case, which means the complete path is built in O(&#119899;) time complexity.\nWhen you implement this change, make sure to store the letters in the path as if you were traversing it from A to B.\n","excerpt":"final_path.insert is not efficient, it has a O(&#119899;) time complexity where &#119899; is the size of <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>. &hellip; ","title":"BFS implementation takes too much time for CSES labyrinth problem"},{"tags":["rust","vector"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":77822244,"is_answered":false,"question_id":77822201,"item_type":"answer","score":2,"last_activity_date":1705351306,"creation_date":1705351306,"body":"You can use the  and  methods (see this playground):\n\nWith the input , this produces .\n","excerpt":"Path::strip_prefix methods (see this playground):\nlet parent = path.parent().expect(&quot;path has no parent&quot;);\nlet first_component = path.components().first().expect(&quot;path has no first component&quot;);\nlet <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to remove the first and the last item of a path components in Rust?"},{"tags":["c++","windows"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":77773189,"is_answered":false,"question_id":77772925,"item_type":"answer","score":5,"last_activity_date":1704633213,"creation_date":1704633213,"body":"The strategy is to open the file and retrieve the file path that is actually opened (With ):\n\nIt is crucial not to open the file with  because then the case-insensitive path translation won&#39;t happen. You don&#39;t need read/write permissions on the file for this. This will fail if the path doesn&#39;t exist, in which case you might want to traverse the subdirectories to see if they are in the correct case.\nI think this is what  does with the Microsoft STL as well.\n\nAlternatively, you can implement the Lua  on Windows to open with . This will not allow you to read  when  was written to and allows you to have two different files  and , even on Windows.\n","excerpt":"to open the file and retrieve the file path that is actually opened (With GetFinalPathNameByHandle):\nstruct HandleCloser {\n    HANDLE h;\n    ~HandleCloser() { ::CloseHandle(h); }\n};\n\nstd::wstring <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Is there a function that gets the true, case sensitive path of a file on Windows?"},{"tags":["python","django","selenium-webdriver","python-unittest"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":77675820,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1703077581,"creation_date":1702843111,"body":"When I pass my test in Django the result shows an output errors that looks like that:\n\nThe code I am using for the test is:\n\nThe final output says that all the test are OK, so I assume that my test is working properly but these errors annoy me and I don&#39;t find information about it.\n","excerpt":"/views/static.py&quot;, line 34, in serve\n    fullpath = Path(safe_join(document_root, path))\n  File &quot;/Users/bav/venv/django/lib/python3.9/site-packages/django/utils/_os.py&quot;, line 17, in safe_join\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Errors testing in Django with Selenium"},{"tags":["python","web-scraping","scrapy","captcha"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":56349371,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1702030603,"creation_date":1559073903,"body":"I&#39;m creating a crawler to Amazon for study reason, but it is being caught by their captcha.\nSo I have made a captcha solver, but I&#39;m having trouble to response the captcha form.\nThe problem is that if I put a yeild FormRequest in the method, it seems to not be called.\n\n\n\nbelow the Amazon&#39;s captcha form\n\n\n\nI put two logs into the code.\n\nThe first on inside the verify_if_captcha:\n\n\n\nThis one is printed\n\nThe second inside the solve_captcha:\n\n\n\nThis one is never printed\n\nCan some one help me, please?\n","excerpt":"pd.read_csv(&#39;scraps/&#39; + rank_path)\n    start_urls = lista_links[&#39;links&#39;].values\n    custom_settings = {&#39;FEED_URI&#39;: &quot;scraps/produtos_%(time)s.csv&quot;,\n                       &#39;FEED_FORMAT&#39;: &#39;csv&#39;}\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Trying to response Amazon&#39;s Captcha with scrapy, strange behavior on spider generator"},{"tags":["python","django","ubuntu","gunicorn","digital-ocean"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":2,"is_answered":true,"question_id":68515723,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1701109687,"creation_date":1627190483,"body":"I am following this tutorial on DigitalOcean for the setup of a Django site. After initial setup and migrations, when I test the usual  it works well (showing all images and grabbing all static files) with no errors.\nWhen I test , no static files are grabbed (anyway, I understand that&#39;s Nginx&#39;s job) but this error appears for every static file requested in the page:\n\nI suspect this is related to , where  are declared as a list, according to Django:\n\nThis doesn&#39;t look to me related to  (but it could be).\nWhy is it that there is no error in the Django server and when Gunicorn is used suddenly the list of paths is a problem?\nI&#39;m using Python 3.8.10, Django 3.2.5, gunicorn 20.1.0 and nginx 1.18.0 on Ubuntu 20.04.2 LTS.\n","excerpt":"views/static.py&quot;, line 36, in serve\n    fullpath = Path(safe_join(document_root, path))\n  File &quot;/home/tsg/buses/busesenv/lib/python3.8/site-packages/django/utils/_os.py&quot;, line 17, in safe_join\n    <span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span> &hellip; ","title":"Gunicorn returns &quot;TypeError: expected str, bytes or os.PathLike object, not list&quot; when testing Django site in Ubuntu server"},{"tags":["python","ssh","paramiko"],"question_score":10,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":4,"is_answered":true,"question_id":23144286,"item_type":"question","score":10,"last_activity_date":1699539598,"creation_date":1397772424,"body":"The Question:\n\nCan I do something like: \n\n\n\nI can make folders, but not files? Do I need to explicitly call sudo or set something in paramiko? Should I be copying the file to a permissable space and chowning? Is there a way to give paramikko sudoer without using keys or having to mess around with ? What am I missing?\n\nThe Code:\n\n\n\nThe Traceback:\n\n\n\nOther posts I&#39;ve looked at:\n\n\nhttp://www.lag.net/paramiko/docs/paramiko.SFTPClient-class.html#put\nI&#39;m trying to understand why I&#39;m getting a &quot;Permission Denied&quot; error when using paramiko 1.7.6\n&#39;Put&#39; in SFTP using PAramiko\nIOError: [Errno 13] Permission denied:\nIOError: [Errno 13] Permission denied\nWhy am I getting IOError: [Errno 13] Permission denied?\nPython - IOError: [Errno 13] Permission denied:\nusing shutil.copyfile I get a Python IOError: [Errno 13] Permission denied:\nhttps://github.com/fabric/fabric/issues/257\nhttps://github.com/fabric/fabric/issues/828\nhttps://github.com/fabric/fabric/issues/257\nhttp://code.activestate.com/recipes/576810-copy-files-over-ssh-using-paramiko/\nHow to run sudo with paramiko? (Python)\n\n\nSome of the posts are sort of old, but seemed to indicate that paramiko doesn&#39;t have it implemented? Fabric has a version implemented, but I am not sure about adding more dependencies. \n\n\n","excerpt":"= posixpath.join(destination,destinationFolder)\n\n                try:\n                    self.sftp.mkdir(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>, mode=777)\n                except:\n                    print(traceback.format_exc() &hellip; )\n                final_destination = posixpath.join(<span class=\"highlight\">final_path</span>, filename)\n                sourceFilePath = os.path.join(cwd,source)\n                print(&#39;\\n# Source Path: {}\\n# Destination Path: {}\\n &hellip; ","title":"Paramiko Python: IOError: [Errno 13] Permission denied"},{"tags":["python","django"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":77162489,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1695897994,"creation_date":1695462969,"body":"I&#39;m running into an issue on my Django app. The issue is when I click on  the app returns the error:  but it shouldn&#39;t since I&#39;m not doing any joining in my code. The view for this is:\n\nI found a post here (I forgot to save the link) where the answer was to remove  from my code but the form doesn&#39;t show up on the page when I removed it\nThe traceback is:\n\n","excerpt":"safe_join(template_dir, template_name)\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File &quot;C:\\Users\\admin\\DEV\\job-tracker\\venv\\Lib\\site-packages\\django\\utils\\_os.py&quot;, line 17, in safe_join\n    